rtainly connected in terms of  philanthropy and resources you know I  think so you're giving us an encouraging  word is that I think that it's a little  bit like the I think it's the comparable  the mastered see that's important  yesterday we received a  twenty-five-thousand-dollar grant from  Los Angeles not known as the center of  philanthropy while the center of  activism from an indian-american  individual came out of the blue it's you  know it will not only sustain it will  develop our program and lead to we  believe some additional larger scale  government assistance a two major forms  of participating in development one is  private philanthropy and there's a  plenty of good groups here today that  are active in that area and the other is  government philanthropy if I can use  that word development assistance and the  two need to go together I think we do  need to be challenged i think we  certainly need to be doing better in  both scales both on the private side and  on the governmental side well certainly  we need a partnership and those who  think just one or the other is the  answer i think all of us in this room  would object to that Katherine did you  have something one of the most powerful  messages that cuts across all of the  religious traditions in one way or  another is the sense of the of the value  the gift of each individual and the the  human human dignity the the love of  neighbor with that I think somehow where  we we need to focus is on translating  these horrors and the  numbers into human faces and families  what does what does maternal mortality  mean what does child mortality mean so  of course it is the hitting people in  the face with with the enormity of the  problems and it's poignancy and  appealing to people's hearts but I think  it has to come with this sense of hope  that that these are problems that can be  overcome that have been overcome in many  places that we've overcome them in this  country and then of course some we're  tailing at the end is the fear factor  that if we don't there is no world for  our children and grandchildren because  there is no no safety you know in a  divided world where people are angry and  unjust so we're balancing this sense of  humanity the the sense of hope the sense  of conscience that comes from from being  part of a family and then somewhere we  we need to convey this sense that it is  not a viable sustainable safe world  unless we are able to work for justice  and development unless people have jobs  go to school and have hope Thank You  Catherine I appreciate what you and  others have said in writing and in your  words to encourage us that yes this is a  new day and we can see the progress made  on the Millennium Development Goals and  so we can have hope this is not hopeless  Melinda um I wanted to maybe end with a  site on hope hopeful side as well on the  and on the political side I mean I was  pretty harsh about that perhaps earlier  but I had a meeting with the new chair  of the House Financial Services  Committee which is Spencer Bachus who is  a Republican from Birmingham Alabama  with a dozen faith leaders from  Birmingham  had a meeting with him and he has been a  stalwart champion of debt relief  initiatives because the faith committee  community in Birmingham reached out to  him and you know 1999 David Beckman was  very involved in that as well and and so  we were strategizing with him you know I  did say I know that this is a different  fiscal environment this is a challenging  time and and he said it is challenging  but we can make the argument so they can  hear them he said because it is very  it's so much more efficient and  effective dollar for dollar to be doing  this type of foreign assistance than  sending our military when there's  instability and so forth so he really  strongly feels like we can make that  national security argument is and and  and I think it's important to educate as  ambassador Hall said the new members and  one