ate in the US  has over the years the most effective  advocate for debt relief in the US  Congress he's worked on primary  education in Africa and around the world  he voted for the child nutrition act  that came before Congress dealing with  nutrition programs in this country in  December he's really got a heart for  these things but it really started with  constituents who with Pat Pelham  listening to God's call to her and using  the power that we have as people who  live in this country to change history  for poor people and I'm also quite  hopeful I the world's made progress  against poverty over the last ten years  in fact voter public attitudes toward  poor people in our country around the  world had become more supportive of  government efforts  to reduce poverty I think we have a real  chance right now in the midst of the  recession to get our country and its  government to get more serious about  poverty than we have ever been and that  that's what God's calling us to do this  land no question thank you David Dean  Harold attridge also professor of New  Testament at Yale Divinity School it's  clear as this panel and presentation  tonight have indicated that the  Christian faith indicates that we have  an obligation an obligation to feed the  hungry to give drink to the thirsty to  clothe the naked to shelter the homeless  indeed our ultimate relationship with  our creator and Redeemer depends on how  we take that obligation Jewish and  Christian brothers and sisters have  similar ways of framing the obligation  that comes from religious faith for us  Christians and lent is a time to reflect  to reflect to get sharper focus and to  engage in action as congressman [ __ ]  said I join congressman senator Coons  and the panel here tonight in calling on  all our alums all our friends and all  people of faith in this country to  engage during this time of Lent in a  period of reflection reflection on not  simply personal sin but on the social  sin that is poverty reflection not  simply on the fact of that sin but also  when its causes and the ways in which it  can be dealt with and then I call upon  us all to act to act by giving giving  generously to act by connecting with all  of the organizations or at least some of  the organizations represented here  tonight who are engaged in the the war  on poverty and finally to engage in  vigorous public discourse about what our  obligations are as a nation to deal with  the reality of poverty yale divinity  school stands ready to help that  conversation move forward during the  course of the next 40 days through our  website through facebook and through  twitter all sorts of interesting new  media will be engaging in highlighting  best practices and highlighting what's  being done by our friends and  our partners in this whole effort so I  invite you to sign up for facebook and  join us in that brave new electronic  world while you're doing so let's all  also give thanks to those who've made  this program possible tonight to Senator  Coons and to our distinguished panel to  reverent later for giving the leadership  on this panel tonight and to all of the  staff of the international relief and  development agency and Yale Divinity  School who pull this panel together  thank you one and all for being here  with us tonight and thank you
 

